WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.679
Miyamoto Musashi was the greatest swordsman
to ever come out a nation of great swordsmen

00:00:21.679 --> 00:00:23.360
- Japan.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.650
His story, which has become the basis for
countless flying swordsman Oriental movies,

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:33.480
is one of raw courage, unbridled ambition
and unparalleled mindfulness.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:39.600
In one on one combat he defeated 60 opponents,
yet his greatest legacy is his seminal work,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.680
the Book of Five Rings, which is still pored
over my military tacticians and mindfulness

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.110
enthusiasts 450 years after it was written.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:54.350
In this week’s Biographics, we reveal the
man who was Musashi.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.460
Beginnings
There is much uncertainty over the details

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.200
and dates pertaining to the life of Musashi.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.640
What follows is based primarily on his own
account as recorded in The Book of Five Rings.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.740
Miyamoto Musashi was born in the spring of
1584 in the village of Miyamoto in the Yoshino

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.470
District of Mimisaka Province in Japan.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:17.670
He was born into nobility, with his mother,
who died giving birth to him, being the daughter

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.680
of a minor local chieftain.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.630
His father, Muni was a warrior in the Shin
Man clan, who had become an expert in the

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:24.640
fighting arts.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.100
He was especially adept in jiu jitsu, swordsmanship
and the use of the jitta, an iron rod with

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.960
forklike protrusions.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.930
When he was in his thirties, Muni had been
invited to the capital of Kyoto to duel with

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:39.009
another highly regarded warrior in the presence
of the Shogun.

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:41.720
Muni beat the man in two out of three bouts.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:47.409
The victory established Musashi’s father
as He Ho Shou (Without Equal) in Japan.

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:51.640
Muni was intent on teaching his son everything
that he knew about the fighting arts.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.020
Although Musashi was a quick learner, he had
a stubborn streak that saw him constantly

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.189
clashing with his father.

00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:01.439
Whereas everyone else treated Muni with the
utmost respect and reverence, Musashi would

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:04.110
often talk back and criticize his father.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.550
This resulted in a volatile relationship which
often culminated in violence.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.549
On one occasion, Muni took a knife and threw
it at his son.

00:02:11.549 --> 00:02:15.200
Musashi managed to dodge the weapon,which
buried itself in the wall beside him.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.900
But the father’s anger was not over - he
threw the seven year-old boy out of his house,

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.450
essentially disowning him.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.090
Musashi crossed the Kamasaka pass into the
province of Harima where he traveled to the

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.810
small village of Hirafuku.

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.930
Here he was taken in by his uncle Dorin.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:33.080
Dorin had once been a warrior but had long
ago renounced the fighting arts, becoming

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.440
a Buddhist monk and living a life of study
and meditation.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:40.490
He raised Musashi at his small Buddhist temple,
set on the edge of the village.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:46.569
Here the boy learned to read and write as
well as to meditate and develop his spirituality.

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:51.611
The Fighter Emerges
Musashi’s real passion, however, lay with

00:02:51.611 --> 00:02:52.670
the martial arts.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.879
He spent countless hours alone in the woods,
sparring against the unforgiving trunks of

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:01.890
pine trees, his anger at his father being
released as he built upon the skills that

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.989
had been instilled by that great warrior.

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:08.470
In his great work The Book of Five Rings,
Musashi recorded that his first real fight

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:10.569
occurred when he was thirteen years of age.

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:15.040
He related that a passing warrior by the name
of Arima Kihei had put up a sign along the

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.420
Sayo river challenging any local swordsman
to a duel.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:23.330
The bold and impetuous Musashi took up the
challenge, writing his name on the sign.

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:27.610
Kihei considered the challenge a joke, but
accepted it anyway, intent on teaching the

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.400
young upstart a lesson.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.379
Musashi’s uncle, Dorin, was shocked when
he heard what his nephew had done.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:36.580
On the day of the challenge, he went ahead
of the boy and attempted to beg off.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:41.810
While he was pleading with Kihei, Musashi
charged his opponent with a six foot quarterstaff.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.900
He caught Kihei off guard, clubbing him to
the ground and then striking him between the

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:46.900
eyes.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.950
With a succession of blows he then beat him
to death.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:54.010
Three years later, Musashi left Hirafuku and
set off to make his way in the world.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:57.840
He traveled along the Sanyodo, the old high
road skirting the northern shore of the Inland

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.200
Sea to the straits of Shimonoseki.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:05.810
From there he ferried across the waters to
the port of Kokura and on to Nakatsu, the

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.849
castle town where his father now lived.

00:04:07.849 --> 00:04:11.920
Muni had entered the service of Yoshitaka,
the master of Nakatsu Castle.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:15.470
The teenager arrived to find that his father’s
army were in the midst of a campaign to subdue

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.419
the island of Kyshu and wage war on the western
provinces.

00:04:19.419 --> 00:04:24.190
Musashi was recruited into the force and soon
found himself fighting alongside his father.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.320
It was his first taste of war.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.060
The campaign proved to be a great success,
with two provinces being brought under the

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.190
dominion of Yoshitaka.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.281
Following the campaign, Musashi’s aging
father retired from active duty, moving to

00:04:36.281 --> 00:04:38.250
the nearby port of Kitsuki.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:44.290
Yet as the father exited from the world of
mortal combat, the son was just getting started.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:47.770
The campaign had wet his appetite and he was
eager for more.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:52.560
Intent on becoming a great warrior, or swordsman,
like his father he set out a path forward.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.580
He needed to perfect his art of swordsmanship,
seek out and defeat other masters and then

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:00.720
found his own school of swordsmanship.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.260
The Challenger
Never one to think small, Musashi set his

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:10.530
sights on the most prestigious school of swordsmanship
in the realm - the famous Yoshioka clan in

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:11.530
Kyoto.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:15.680
He knew that if he could fully defeat even
one member of this clan, he would put himself

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.020
on the fast-track to recognition as a master
swordsman.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.080
So, without saying a word to his father, he
rose early one morning, collected his few

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:26.039
belongings and set out to claim his destiny.

00:05:26.039 --> 00:05:30.240
He bartered his way onto a small merchant
vessel to the port of Sakaki.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.720
From there he traveled on foot to Kyoto and
sought out the Yoshioka clan.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.680
He threw down his challenge, which was eagerly
accepted by their top swordsman, Seijuro.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.190
The duel would take place on the grounds of
the Rendai temple.

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:46.030
When he arrived at the venue, Musashi leapt
upon his opponent without warning - just as

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:51.060
he had in his first duel - and felled Seijuro
with a blow from his bokuto.

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.979
The fight was over before it had properly
begun.

00:05:53.979 --> 00:05:57.419
The shock defeat of their top swordsman rocked
the Yoshioka clan.

00:05:57.419 --> 00:06:02.320
Seijuro’s brother, Denshichiro, was incensed
at the manner in which the young upstart from

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:05.070
the south had attacked his brother when he
was unprepared.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:09.540
He challenged Musashi to a second challenge
on the very spot where the first had occurred.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:13.630
This time it was the Yoshioka swordsman who
charged first, attempting to strike a fatal

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:15.479
blow with his five-foot bokuto.

00:06:15.479 --> 00:06:20.199
But Musashi managed to wrestle the weapon
away from Denshichiro, using it to beat his

00:06:20.199 --> 00:06:21.570
opponent to death.

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:25.630
The Yoshioka had now been bested twice in
short order.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:30.090
This was too much for their honor to bare,
and they decided that Musashi must be killed.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:34.120
They devised a plan to ambush him in the woods
outside of the hostel he was staying at.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:38.130
More than a hundred Yoshioka students, armed
with sticks, bows and arrows gathered under

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:44.240
the leadership of Denshichiro’s son, Matashichiro
came up against Musashi.

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:49.470
Unperturbed, Musashi rushed straight for Matashichiro,
killing him in front of the others.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:53.770
Overawed, the Yoshioka fell back, allowing
Musashi to slip away.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:58.699
The Supreme Warrior
Having established his supremacy by decimating

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:03.130
the most highly respected warrior clan in
the land, Musashi returned to Kyoto.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:07.009
In the space of a few days he had brought
down a school of swordsmanship that had reigned

00:07:07.009 --> 00:07:08.979
supreme for over a century.

00:07:08.979 --> 00:07:12.860
Still, he knew that he had a lot to learn
in order to became the greatest warrior in

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:13.860
the nation.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.620
From Kyoto, he traveled to the temple to Nara.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:19.979
Here he found a line of warrior monks who
were experts in Hozo’in-ryu, the art of

00:07:19.979 --> 00:07:21.389
fighting with the lance.

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:25.919
The Temple master was fascinated with Musashi’s
ability to fight with two swords at once.

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:29.771
Keen to test his finest student against the
newcomer, he arranged for two bouts with a

00:07:29.771 --> 00:07:31.870
monk named Okuzo’in.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:35.789
In neither was the lancer able to gain the
upper hand over Musashi.

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:40.470
Leaving Nara, Musashi traveled to Edo, a city
that was teeming with accomplished swordsmen,

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:43.880
many of whom had forged their talents on the
field of battle.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:47.330
Here Musashi opened his own dojo, one of hundreds
in the city.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:51.060
News quickly spread of his unique style, and
soon he was being flooded with students.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:55.530
One of them was Mizuno Katsunari, a well respected
and battle hardened warrior.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:08:01.250
The fame of Musashi’s dojo spread, reaching
all the way to Edo Castle and the Shogun himself.

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:04.590
He was invited to teach there, but declined
when he realized that he would be subject

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:07.180
to a member of the hated Yagyu clan.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:11.440
After teaching in Edo for seven years, Musashi
decided to return to Kitsuki, the town of

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:12.440
his father.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:16.319
The aging Muni had set up his own dojo and
was eager to pass it on to his son.

00:08:16.319 --> 00:08:21.370
Before he arrived back in Kitsuki, however,
Musashi, was to fight one more challenger.

00:08:21.370 --> 00:08:26.150
His journey home brought him to the small
island of Funashima, in the straits of Shimonoseki.

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:31.330
It was here that he was challenged by a famed
warrior by the name of Sasaki Kojiro, a man

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:35.450
so fierce that he had earned the moniker ‘Demon
of the Western Provinces.’

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:38.890
He was a master of nodachi, the great Japanese
sword.

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:43.010
Legend tells us that Musashi’s only weapon
was a bokken, or wooden sword, that he had

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:46.270
carved from an oar of the boat that had brought
him to the island.

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:48.530
With this he managed to subdue and kill Sasaki.

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:53.370
He then immediately jumped into his boat and
rowed out to sea in order to get away from

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:56.620
Sasaki’s enraged followers.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:00.870
Warrior
Musashi now completed his journey to Kitsuki

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:04.950
where he began teaching at his father’s
dojo.For three years he lived and worked in

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.070
the small seaside village.

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:10.250
During this time his fractured relationship
with his father got no better, with the old

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:13.940
man criticizing his son’s martial ability
at every opportunity.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:19.040
When Muni died at the age of 85, Musashi was
more relieved than saddened.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.340
Shortly after his father’s passing, Musashi
received a letter from his former student,

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:24.340
Mizuno Katsunari.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:28.930
War had broken out between the great clans
of Tokugawa and Toyotomi.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:33.769
Katsunari was about to take part in the siege
on the Tokugawa side along with his eldest

00:09:33.769 --> 00:09:36.010
son, who was just 16 years old.

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:40.570
Katsunari requested that Musashi serve as
a part of a special escort of ten mounted

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:42.410
warriors for his son.

00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:47.070
The thirty year old Musashi saw this as his
opportunity to jump back into the thick of

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:48.070
combat.

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:50.070
He set off from Kitsuki for the port of Sakaki.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:54.120
He was determined to be the protector in battle
of the young son of his beloved student, Katsunari.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.230
He met up with Katsunari’s forces, riding
at the head of 4,000 men as they advanced

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:03.570
on Osaka and then on to Kokobu, a hamlet made
up of a few farms stretching along the southern

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:05.750
bank of the Yamamoto river.

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.800
Just a few miles downstream a large enemy
force had gathered.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:13.230
That night, however, Katsunari’s army managed
to surround the Toyotomi forces.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:18.029
The attack was ordered at 10 pm, the hour
of the snake, and the Toyotomi were sent reeling.

00:10:18.029 --> 00:10:22.160
They fled across the Ishi river, only to be
chased down by Katsunari’s men.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.829
Musashi was in the lead, wielding his favorite
weapon, the bokutu.

00:10:25.829 --> 00:10:29.560
Records tell us that he stood on the heights
of a bridge, sending one enemy warrior after

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:33.519
another flying with the deadly blows of his
trusty bokutu.

00:10:33.519 --> 00:10:38.500
The Osaka winter and summer campaigns, in
which the Tokugawa were victorious, put an

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:41.970
end to the resistance of Japan’s western
warlords.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:45.690
The culmination of the hostilities was the
siege of Osaka castle.

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:50.550
It was the largest battle in which Musashi
would ever take part, with many thousands

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:52.779
of warriors being slaughtered on both sides.

00:10:52.779 --> 00:10:59.230
Among those of his comrades to fall was Nakagawa
Shimanosuke, who was stricken down as Katsunari’s

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:01.330
men had stormed the southern gate of the castle.

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:04.220
This man had three sons who traveled with
the army.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:06.160
The two older boys took part in the siege.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:08.050
But the youngest was just eleven.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:11.750
With the death of his father, he now needed
a guardian.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:16.440
Father and Mentor
Musashi took the boy, Mikinosuke under his

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:21.450
wing, adopting him as his son, and set off
for the castle town of Himeji, then on to

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:23.790
Hirafuku, where he step-mother lived.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:27.890
For the next two years he lived here, caring
for his aged step-mother while also providing

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:29.209
for his new son.

00:11:29.209 --> 00:11:33.880
Many hours each day were spent imparting to
Mikinosuke the fighting skills that he himself

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:36.810
had learned as a young boy from his own father.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:40.540
This tranquil existence was interrupted when
news reached the village of Hirafuku that

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:45.950
Ikeda Mitsumasa, the current lord of Himeji
castle, was going to move.

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:48.470
The new lord was going to be Honda Tadamasa.

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:52.330
Musashi saw this change in leadership as an
opportunity for his adopted son.

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:57.040
During the storming of the Osaka castle, Tadamasa
had been in command of the second eastern

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:01.459
phalanx, directly behind the troops of Katsunari,
where Musashi was stationed.

00:12:01.459 --> 00:12:04.959
He had, therefore, seen Musashi in action
in the heat of the battle.

00:12:04.959 --> 00:12:09.750
Musashi requested that his son Mitsumasa be
given a duty in the lord’s household.

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:13.529
Tadamasa agreed and the boy was made a page
to his own son, Tadatoki.

00:12:13.529 --> 00:12:18.790
At the same time, Musashi was hired by the
neighboring daimyo, Ogasawa Tadazane.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:23.329
He was appointed as an advisor to the magistrate
in charge of construction of a huge building

00:12:23.329 --> 00:12:25.889
project, the construction of Akashi Castle.

00:12:25.889 --> 00:12:28.399
This was followed with the planning of the
town of Himeji.

00:12:28.399 --> 00:12:32.570
Musashi was also put in charge of the castle
gardens, and oversaw the construction of a

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:35.180
teahouse, miniature mountains and miniature
lake.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:40.240
He proved to have a meticulous eye for detail
and was obsessive in getting every tree, shrub

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:42.250
and stone placed just right.

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:45.529
The years that he spent at Akashi were the
happiest of Musashi’s life.

00:12:45.529 --> 00:12:49.730
As well as planning out the landscaping and
tending to the gardens, he instructed his

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:52.470
retainers in the art of fighting with two
swords.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:55.010
He also obtained much satisfaction in hearing
of the successes of his adopted son, Mikinosuke,

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:59.590
who grew in favor in the household of Tadamasa.

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:03.480
Tragedy
In the spring off 1626, Lord Tadatoki was

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:08.279
struck down with tuberculosis, being forced
to keep to his quarters at Himeji castle.

00:13:08.279 --> 00:13:12.269
After a three month struggle with the disease,
he died, aged 30.

00:13:12.269 --> 00:13:14.970
Musashi knew all too well what this meant
for his son.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:19.620
As the key retainer of the stricken Lord,
Mikinosuke was customarily bound to commit

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.149
seppuku, ritual suicide by disembowelment.

00:13:22.149 --> 00:13:26.970
Mikinosuke’s death left a deep emotional
scar on Musashi from which he would never

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:28.040
recover.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:32.209
For months he shunned all company, retreating
to the forest to commune with nature and try

00:13:32.209 --> 00:13:34.480
to come to terms with his great loss.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:39.180
Almost exactly a year after Mikinosuke’s
death, Musashi adopted a second son - a boy

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:43.880
named Iori, who was the second son of Tamara
Hisamitsu, a Samurai in the service of the

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:45.389
lord of Miki castle.

00:13:45.389 --> 00:13:49.820
When that castle had been overthrown by the
Tokugawa army, Hisamitsu took up life as a

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:50.820
poor farmer.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.490
When he died, his younger son, Iori, became
an orphan.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:57.529
Musashi took him in and arranged for him to
enter the service of Lord Ogasawara Tadazane.

00:13:57.529 --> 00:14:01.140
The boy would eventually attain the rank of
senior retainer, stationed on the southern

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:02.769
island of Kyushu.

00:14:02.769 --> 00:14:06.050
Musashi moved there to be closer to the boy
and was to spend the last twenty years of

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:07.480
his life on the island.

00:14:07.480 --> 00:14:11.800
Musashi built a large dojo on the island,
and soon had a huge following, all eager to

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:13.709
learn from the great master.

00:14:13.709 --> 00:14:18.170
Even though located on a remote island far
from the main centers, many martial arts masters

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:22.730
visited his dojo to experience the art of
fighting with two swords first hand.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:27.570
The aging Musashi was respected and admired
as a martial arts grand master.

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:30.350
Still he was, as he had always been, an outsider.

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:32.720
He did not groom himself as was expected.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:37.600
He left his hair grown long and unkempt, seldom
bathed and refused to wear the traditional

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:39.310
attire of a swordsman of that time.

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:43.949
In his waning years, he even abandoned his
two swords, preferring to carry and use nothing

00:14:43.949 --> 00:14:46.180
more than a five foot wooden staff.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:50.240
In early 1638 an armed revolt broke out in
the province of Hizen.

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:55.329
The rebellion was made up of Christians, led
by 17-year-old Amakusa Shiro, and numbered

00:14:55.329 --> 00:14:56.520
more than 20,000 men.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:00.589
They had ensconced themselves the long abandoned
Hara castle.

00:15:00.589 --> 00:15:05.519
Lord Ogasawara Tadazane, the employer of Musashi’s
adopted son, engaged the aging master to escort

00:15:05.519 --> 00:15:09.470
his 23-year old son to the battlefield, along
with 8,000 men.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:12.699
Musashi stayed at the boy’s side throughout
the entire siege.

00:15:12.699 --> 00:15:16.490
When the defenders inside the castle threw
boulders down from the walls, he leapt in

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:20.220
front of the lord’s horse and fended them
off with his staff.

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:24.101
The Final Years
Musashi hadn’t been back from the Hizen

00:15:24.101 --> 00:15:27.339
rebellion for long when he was approached
to take up a position in the household of

00:15:27.339 --> 00:15:31.850
the daimyo of one of the largest fiefs in
the country, Hosokawa Tadatoshi.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:36.699
Tadatoshi wanted Musashi to relocate to Kumamoto
and instruct his retainers in the art fighting

00:15:36.699 --> 00:15:37.889
with two swords.

00:15:37.889 --> 00:15:41.779
The swordsman accepted, arriving in Kumamoto
in September, 1640.

00:15:41.779 --> 00:15:45.750
He was given a comfortable mansion on the
northeastern side of the castle grounds.

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:49.850
Musashi took to spending much time in the
mountains of Iwato, where he would wile away

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:52.180
the hours in meditation and contemplation.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:54.740
He found a cave which afforded him perfect
solitude.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:56.820
It was here that he began to put his thoughts
to paper

00:15:56.820 --> 00:16:00.980
In the early Spring of 1645 he completed the
first draft of the work that would make him

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:06.120
immortal down through the ages - the Gorin
no Sho, or Book of Five Rings.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:10.700
The book had taken five seasons to complete,
each one seeing the completion of one long

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:11.700
scroll.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:15.199
He named them the scrolls of Earth, Fire,
Water, Wind and Heaven.

00:16:15.199 --> 00:16:18.820
Shortly after the completion of his great
work, Musashi’s health took a sudden turn

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:19.820
for the worse.

00:16:19.820 --> 00:16:21.880
Within a month, he was no longer able to teach.

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:24.279
He withdrew to his house outside the castle
walls.

00:16:24.279 --> 00:16:28.320
Feeling his life ebbing away, he summoned
his chief retainer and handed him the Gorin

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:29.410
no Sho.

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:33.920
Having passed on his legacy he then closed
his eyes and allowed the life to drain from

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:34.920
his body.

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:37.269
It was June the 13th, 1645.

00:16:37.269 --> 00:16:41.699
Shortly after his death, a stone memorial,
to be known as Mound Musashi, was erected

00:16:41.699 --> 00:16:43.220
not far from his home in Kaido.

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:48.300
For centuries thereafter passersby would dismount
from their horses to pay homage to the greatest

00:16:48.300 --> 00:17:08.020
swordsman that Japan would ever produce.

